// TEST: Struct Static Bound Forward Ref
// ============================================================================
// THE CONSTRUCTION-PATH TWIN of index-types/146, pinning that a field bound
// naming a `let static` creates a STATIC DEPENDENCY that the resolver honors.
//
// `Plain { m = 2 }` mentions `Plain` and `m` and NO static, so the dependency
// graph cannot see that `zz_lim` must be resolved first by reading the folding
// expression alone. The edge has to be learned from the TYPE, by pulling in
// the statics that its field bounds and declared conjuncts name. That edge was
// missing once; this file is what says so if it goes missing again. It is also
// a SEPARATE SEED from the consumption path (`idx_card`), so the two can
// regress independently and both need pinning.
//
// THE NAMES ARE THE TEST. Read this before renaming anything below.
//
// Static resolution drains a Map in KEY order. A missing dependency edge is
// therefore INVISIBLE whenever the depended-on static happens to sort before
// the binding that consumes it — key order alone gets it right, and the file
// passes with the edge logic deleted. Source order does not save you either:
// the resolver does not read top-to-bottom, so declaring the static last is
// necessary and nowhere near sufficient.
//
// So the consumers are named `a_*` and the static `zz_lim`: the consumers sort
// FIRST under any comparer (ordinal, ordinal-ignore-case, culture-aware), so
// without the edge they fold before `zz_lim` exists and fail with "undefined
// variable 'zz_lim'". An earlier draft of this file used `LO` with consumers
// `top`/`bot` — under ordinal comparison uppercase precedes lowercase, so `LO`
// drained first and the file proved nothing about the mechanism it was
// written for. It passed for the wrong reason, which is the failure mode this
// header exists to prevent recurring.
//
// DO NOT rename these bindings to anything descriptive, and do not tidy the
// `zz_lim` declaration upward. Both are load-bearing and both are invisible.
//
// The negative half is pinned separately (diagnostics/032, same naming
// discipline): resolving the bound is not the same as ENFORCING it, and a fix
// that restored visibility while dropping the conjunct would pass this file.
// ============================================================================
struct Plain {
    m: Int<min=-zz_lim, max=zz_lim>
}

// Folds in the compile-time world, at the top of the range. Sorts before
// `zz_lim` in key order, so only a real dependency edge can order this right.
let static a_top = Plain { m = 2 }
let a = a_top.m
// EXPECT: a = 2

// And at the bottom — `min=-zz_lim` needs the same edge through a unary minus.
let static a_bot = Plain { m = -2 }
let b = a_bot.m
// EXPECT: b = -2

// The static everything above depends on: declared last, and named to sort
// last. Neither property is cosmetic.
let static zz_lim = 2

// The ordinary runtime path over the same declaration, for contrast: this one
// never enters the compile-time world, so it exercises the synthesized guard
// against a bound whose value still had to be resolved statically.
let mid = Plain { m = 1 }
let c = mid.m
// EXPECT: c = 1
